1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,957
This is ECE 320, video 15. It is part 2 of caches: misses, writes and performance.

2
00:00:08,407 --> 00:00:13,485
After this video, you can compare three ways to access the tags and the data.

3
00:00:13,935 --> 00:00:19,076
You can classify misses with the 3 C model, and use the A B C rules.

4
00:00:19,526 --> 00:00:23,942
You can explain the write policies, the write buffer and the store buffer.

5
00:00:24,392 --> 00:00:28,851
You can calculate the average access time of a cache hierarchy.

6
00:00:29,301 --> 00:00:36,682
And you can calculate the CPI of a machine with cache misses, as in assignment 5 and a past quiz.

7
00:00:37,132 --> 00:00:45,537
So far, a frame held its tag and its data together. In real hardware, the tags are in their own array: the tag array.

8
00:00:45,987 --> 00:00:51,235
The example cache is 4-way. Each set has 4 ways: the 4 columns.

9
00:00:51,685 --> 00:01:00,389
It has 4 sets: the 4 rows. 4 sets need a 2-bit index: 0 0, 0 1, 1 0 and 1 1.

10
00:01:00,839 --> 00:01:08,711
The data blocks are in a second, physically separate array: the data array. It has the same 4 sets and 4 ways.

11
00:01:09,161 --> 00:01:15,540
There are three ways to access the two arrays. Serial access: first the tags, then the data.

12
00:01:15,990 --> 00:01:20,086
Parallel access: the tags and the data at the same time.

13
00:01:20,536 --> 00:01:26,360
Way prediction: it tries to get the best of both. The next three slides show each one.

14
00:01:26,810 --> 00:01:36,303
Serial tag access matches the tag first. The 2-bit index selects a set in the tag array. The index is 0 1, so set 1.

15
00:01:36,753 --> 00:01:43,281
The cache reads the 4 tags of set 1. The comparators compare them with the tag of the address.

16
00:01:43,731 --> 00:01:50,345
The tag matches in way 2. The comparators give the hit way as a 2-bit number: 1 0.

17
00:01:50,795 --> 00:01:58,368
The index 0 1 and the hit way 1 0 together form a 4-bit block number: 0 1 1 0.

18
00:01:58,818 --> 00:02:10,594
In the data array, each set has 4 blocks. Set 1 holds blocks 4 to 7, and way 2 of set 1 is block 6. So 0 1 1 0 is block 6.

19
00:02:11,044 --> 00:02:14,180
Only block 6 is read from the data array.

20
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:17,425
Then the offset selects the data in block 6.

21
00:02:17,875 --> 00:02:21,800
The advantage of serial access: only one block is read.

22
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:29,077
The disadvantage: it is slow. The data read must wait for the tag match, so the two times add up.

23
00:02:29,527 --> 00:02:35,436
Parallel access sends the index to the tag array and to the data array at the same time.

24
00:02:35,886 --> 00:02:42,691
The data array reads all 4 blocks of set 1, one block per way. Four blocks are transferred.

25
00:02:43,141 --> 00:02:49,648
The tag compare gives the hit way. The hit way selects one of the 4 blocks in a 4-to-1 mux.

26
00:02:50,098 --> 00:02:52,061
Then the offset selects the data.

27
00:02:52,511 --> 00:02:58,185
The advantage: it is faster. The tag read and the data read overlap in time.

28
00:02:58,635 --> 00:03:03,862
The disadvantages: 4 block reads instead of 1, and a 4-to-1 mux.

29
00:03:04,312 --> 00:03:11,736
Way prediction tries to get the best of both. A way predictor guesses the way of the block before the tag compare.

30
00:03:12,186 --> 00:03:16,197
The input of the predictor is the index plus some tag bits.

31
00:03:16,647 --> 00:03:21,340
Its output is only a hint: a predicted way, as a 2-bit number.

32
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:25,822
The index and the predicted way form a 4-bit block number.

33
00:03:26,272 --> 00:03:31,285
The cache reads that one block at once. It does not wait for the tag compare.

34
00:03:31,735 --> 00:03:35,362
In parallel, the tag compare finds the real hit way.

35
00:03:35,812 --> 00:03:42,681
A compare checks the real hit way against the prediction. If they are equal, the data is good: a hit.

36
00:03:43,131 --> 00:03:49,190
If they are not equal, the cache must access again. The extra access looks like a miss.

37
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:56,296
The advantages: fast and low power. Only one block is read, and there is no wait for the tag.

38
00:03:56,746 --> 00:04:03,551
The disadvantage: more misses. Way prediction is about 90 percent accurate for a 2-way cache.

39
00:04:04,001 --> 00:04:08,076
The predictor is updated on a mis-prediction or a replacement.

40
00:04:08,526 --> 00:04:16,505
To improve a cache, first find why each miss happens. The 3 C model sorts each miss into one of three classes.

41
00:04:16,955 --> 00:04:25,061
First test: the block was never accessed before. Then the miss is compulsory, or cold. For example, at startup.

42
00:04:25,511 --> 00:04:36,434
Else, the second test: since the last access to this block, the program accessed at least N other distinct blocks. N is the number of frames in the cache.

43
00:04:36,884 --> 00:04:40,852
If yes, the miss is a capacity miss: the cache is too small.

44
00:04:41,302 --> 00:04:47,617
If no, the miss is a conflict miss. It happens because the associativity is too low.

45
00:04:48,067 --> 00:04:55,811
There is a fourth class, coherence misses. They happen only in shared-memory multiprocessors, in a later lecture.

46
00:04:56,261 --> 00:05:06,778
Apply the 3 C model to assignment 5, question 2. There are 13 accesses. The cache has 8 frames of 16 bytes, so N is 8.

47
00:05:07,228 --> 00:05:13,265
A block has 16 bytes. So the block number is the address without its last hex digit.

48
00:05:13,715 --> 00:05:19,177
In the direct-mapped cache with 8 frames, the frame is the block number modulo 8.

49
00:05:19,627 --> 00:05:22,656
The direct-mapped cache has 10 misses and 3 hits.

50
00:05:23,106 --> 00:05:27,906
8 misses are the first access to their block. They are compulsory misses.

51
00:05:28,356 --> 00:05:36,313
The misses at 2 0 4 and 2 0 6 access block 2 0 again. So they are not compulsory.

52
00:05:36,763 --> 00:05:48,667
Before 2 0 4, the last access to block 2 0 was 2 0 2. Between them, only 3 other blocks were accessed: 1 A, 1 0 and 3 6.

53
00:05:49,117 --> 00:06:01,619
Before 2 0 6, the last access to block 2 0 was 2 0 4. Between them, only 4 other blocks were accessed: 1 1, 1 A, 1 7 and 3 0.

54
00:06:02,069 --> 00:06:07,871
3 and 4 are fewer than N, which is 8. So both misses are conflict misses.

55
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:20,097
The reason: in the direct-mapped cache, blocks 1 0, 2 0 and 3 0 all use frame 0. Blocks 1 0 and 3 0 evicted block 2 0.

56
00:06:20,547 --> 00:06:25,689
So the direct-mapped cache has 10 misses: 8 compulsory plus 2 conflict.

57
00:06:26,139 --> 00:06:36,400
The fully associative cache and the 4-way cache have only 8 misses, all compulsory. More ways removed exactly the 2 conflict misses.

58
00:06:36,850 --> 00:06:42,802
The take-away for the A B Cs. Higher associativity decreases conflict misses.

59
00:06:43,252 --> 00:06:46,153
But higher associativity increases t hit.

60
00:06:46,603 --> 00:06:57,419
A larger block size decreases compulsory misses. One miss brings in more neighbouring bytes: with 32-byte blocks, 32 bytes, not 16.

61
00:06:57,869 --> 00:07:07,491
But a larger block size increases conflict misses. The same 128-byte cache then has only 4 frames, not 8.

62
00:07:07,941 --> 00:07:11,439
The effect of the block size on t hit is negligible.

63
00:07:11,889 --> 00:07:15,324
A larger capacity decreases capacity misses.

64
00:07:15,774 --> 00:07:18,526
But a larger capacity increases t hit.

65
00:07:18,976 --> 00:07:22,539
So every change that lowers the miss rate has a cost.

66
00:07:22,989 --> 00:07:38,498
Conflict misses come from too little associativity. More ways are expensive, and few sets need them. The slide's case: blocks X, Y and Z map to the same 2-way set. The program accesses them again and again.

67
00:07:38,948 --> 00:07:42,084
Without a victim buffer, every access misses.

68
00:07:42,534 --> 00:07:56,123
The set has only 2 ways for 3 blocks. With LRU, the set always evicts the block that comes next. After X, Y and Z, the set holds Y and Z, and the next access is X.

69
00:07:56,573 --> 00:08:04,253
The fix is a victim buffer, V B. It is a small fully associative cache, for example with 8 entries.

70
00:08:04,703 --> 00:08:09,802
Blocks that are kicked out of the I cache or the D cache go into the victim buffer.

71
00:08:10,252 --> 00:08:18,615
On a miss, the cache checks the victim buffer. If the block is there, it goes back into the cache. Else, the normal miss starts.

72
00:08:19,065 --> 00:08:26,062
With the victim buffer, only the first 3 accesses miss. All later accesses hit in the victim buffer.

73
00:08:26,512 --> 00:08:34,853
For example, Z evicts X into the victim buffer. The next access, X, finds X there: a victim buffer hit.

74
00:08:35,303 --> 00:08:43,367
The victim buffer acts as 8 extra ways, shared by all sets. Only a few sets need extra ways at any time.

75
00:08:43,817 --> 00:08:48,767
The buffer is small, so it is very fast. It is very effective in practice.

76
00:08:49,217 --> 00:08:56,150
So far we looked at reads, the loads. Stores write into the cache, and that brings four new issues.

77
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:02,360
Issue 1 is tag and data access: whether a write can go in parallel with the tag check.

78
00:09:02,810 --> 00:09:08,058
Issue 2 is write-through or write-back: when the next level gets the new value.

79
00:09:08,508 --> 00:09:14,140
Issue 3 is write-allocate or write-not-allocate: what happens on a write miss.

80
00:09:14,590 --> 00:09:24,403
Issue 4 is inclusion or exclusion: whether a block of the L 1 is also in the L 2. The next slides take the issues in order.

81
00:09:24,853 --> 00:09:28,181
Reads access the tag and the data in parallel.

82
00:09:28,631 --> 00:09:41,901
For example, a load with tag 7 reads a frame that holds tag 5. The tags do not match, so the data is garbage. That is OK: the load stalls until the good data arrives.

83
00:09:42,351 --> 00:09:46,660
Now a store that reads the tag and writes the data in parallel.

84
00:09:47,110 --> 00:09:57,798
A store with tag 7 writes a frame that holds tag 5. The tags do not match, but the data of block 5 is already overwritten: it is clobbered.

85
00:09:58,248 --> 00:10:02,621
So a write is a two-step process. Step 1: match the tag.

86
00:10:03,071 --> 00:10:05,973
Step 2: write the data into the matching way.

87
00:10:06,423 --> 00:10:13,527
The two steps are pipelined. In cycle 2, store B matches its tag while store A writes its data.

88
00:10:13,977 --> 00:10:22,958
Issue 2: when the new value goes to the next level. Option 1 is write-through: immediately. Every store also writes the next level.

89
00:10:23,408 --> 00:10:26,949
So three stores to one block give three bus writes.

90
00:10:27,399 --> 00:10:29,597
Write-through is conceptually simple.

91
00:10:30,047 --> 00:10:34,911
Its miss latency is uniform: a miss is always 1 transaction.

92
00:10:35,361 --> 00:10:37,899
But write-through needs more bus bandwidth.

93
00:10:38,349 --> 00:10:44,557
Option 2 is write-back: the value goes to the next level only when the block is replaced.

94
00:10:45,007 --> 00:10:48,570
Each block needs an extra bit: the dirty bit, D.

95
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:54,972
A store only writes the cache and sets the dirty bit to 1. There is no bus traffic yet.

96
00:10:55,422 --> 00:11:00,478
So write-back needs minimal bus bandwidth: only dirty blocks are written back.

97
00:11:00,928 --> 00:11:07,051
But its miss latency is not uniform. A clean miss needs 1 transaction: the fill.

98
00:11:07,501 --> 00:11:13,879
A dirty miss needs 2 transactions: first write back the dirty block, then fill the new one.

99
00:11:14,329 --> 00:11:20,793
Issue 3: a write miss. The example is a store to X that misses, and then a load of X.

100
00:11:21,243 --> 00:11:24,678
Write-allocate first fills block X from the next level.

101
00:11:25,128 --> 00:11:28,477
Then it writes the value into block X in the D cache.

102
00:11:28,927 --> 00:11:32,767
The next load of X hits. So there are fewer read misses.

103
00:11:33,217 --> 00:11:35,265
But the fill needs more bandwidth.

104
00:11:35,715 --> 00:11:38,723
Write-allocate is used with write-back caches.

105
00:11:39,173 --> 00:11:45,744
Write-not-allocate only writes the value to the next level. It does not bring block X into the cache.

106
00:11:46,194 --> 00:11:50,674
So the next load of X misses. There are potentially more read misses.

107
00:11:51,124 --> 00:11:53,897
But write-not-allocate uses less bandwidth.

108
00:11:54,347 --> 00:11:56,566
It is used with write-through caches.

109
00:11:57,016 --> 00:12:01,752
A write buffer, W B, sits between the cache and the next level.

110
00:12:02,202 --> 00:12:09,370
With a write-through cache, every store goes into the write buffer. So the processor does not wait for memory.

111
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:18,097
With a write-back cache, the write buffer helps on a dirty miss. The cache must replace dirty block D with new block N.

112
00:12:18,547 --> 00:12:23,369
Step 1: the cache first sends the fill request for N to the next level.

113
00:12:23,819 --> 00:12:29,173
Step 2: while the cache waits, it writes the dirty block D into the write buffer.

114
00:12:29,623 --> 00:12:33,250
Step 3: when N arrives, the cache puts it into the frame.

115
00:12:33,700 --> 00:12:36,985
Step 4: the write buffer writes D to the next level.

116
00:12:37,435 --> 00:12:42,747
So the read, the fill, goes first. The dirty write-back does not delay the miss.

117
00:12:43,197 --> 00:12:47,656
On a read miss, the load needs the data to go on, so it must stall.

118
00:12:48,106 --> 00:12:54,655
On a write miss, technically no instruction waits for the data. So the store does not need to stall.

119
00:12:55,105 --> 00:13:04,769
A store buffer, S B, sits between the processor and the D cache. A store puts its address and value into the store buffer and keeps going.

120
00:13:05,219 --> 00:13:09,571
The store buffer writes the stores into the D cache in the background.

121
00:13:10,021 --> 00:13:17,296
A load must search the store buffer as well as the D cache. The newest value may still be in the store buffer.

122
00:13:17,746 --> 00:13:22,141
The result: the store buffer removes most stalls on write misses.

123
00:13:22,591 --> 00:13:28,415
The first level is split: instructions are in the I cache, and data is in the D cache.

124
00:13:28,865 --> 00:13:37,206
Fetch, F, and memory access, M, use the caches in the same cycle. Two caches avoid the structural hazard.

125
00:13:37,656 --> 00:13:47,811
Two small caches also keep t hit small. One larger unified first-level cache would be slow. A second port would make it even slower.

126
00:13:48,261 --> 00:13:56,879
The second and third levels are unified: instructions and data are in one cache. A unified cache minimizes the miss rate.

127
00:13:57,329 --> 00:14:03,495
There are fewer capacity misses, because space that instructions do not use can hold data.

128
00:14:03,945 --> 00:14:11,155
There are more conflict misses between instructions and data. But the effect is much smaller in large caches.

129
00:14:11,605 --> 00:14:17,451
The I cache and the D cache can miss at the same time. Such a double miss is quite rare.

130
00:14:17,901 --> 00:14:25,005
Issue 4 is about the hierarchy. With inclusion, every block in the L 1 is also in the L 2.

131
00:14:25,455 --> 00:14:30,511
With exclusion, a block is in the L 1 or in the L 2, never in both.

132
00:14:30,961 --> 00:14:38,278
A non-inclusive hierarchy is somewhere in between. It has no strict rule for inclusion or exclusion.

133
00:14:38,728 --> 00:14:41,864
In the example, B and C are in both levels.

134
00:14:42,314 --> 00:14:44,298
But A is only in the L 1.

135
00:14:44,748 --> 00:14:55,116
Recall the memory performance equation. It holds for any memory component M. For M 1, t average is the average time of one access.

136
00:14:55,566 --> 00:15:03,118
Every access pays t hit of M 1. In the picture, each of the 10 accesses has a blue t hit part.

137
00:15:03,568 --> 00:15:10,907
A fraction of the accesses misses: the miss rate of M 1. In the picture, 1 of 10 accesses misses.

138
00:15:11,357 --> 00:15:22,386
Each miss also pays t miss of M 1: the time to bring the data into M 1. Averaged over the 10 accesses, the miss adds 1 tenth of t miss.

139
00:15:22,836 --> 00:15:31,476
In a hierarchy, M 1 gets the data from M 2. So t miss of M 1 is the average access time of M 2.

140
00:15:31,926 --> 00:15:40,246
In the same way, t miss of M 2 is t average of M 3. And t miss of M 3 is t average of M 4.

141
00:15:40,696 --> 00:15:45,133
Substitute. t miss of M 1 becomes t average of M 2.

142
00:15:45,583 --> 00:15:55,695
Then expand t average of M 2 with the same equation. It is t hit of M 2, plus the miss rate of M 2 times t miss of M 2.

143
00:15:56,145 --> 00:16:00,135
Repeat the substitution for every level, down to M 4.

144
00:16:00,585 --> 00:16:15,859
An example with numbers that are not from the slides. L 1: t hit is 1 cycle, and 10 percent of accesses miss. L 2: t hit is 10 cycles, and 20 percent miss. Memory takes 100 cycles.

145
00:16:16,309 --> 00:16:23,264
Draw 100 accesses. 10 percent miss in L 1: that is 10 accesses, the bottom row.

146
00:16:23,714 --> 00:16:30,391
20 percent of these 10 L 1 misses also miss in L 2. So 2 accesses go to memory.

147
00:16:30,841 --> 00:16:36,772
Work from the bottom up. An access to L 2 takes t hit of L 2: 10 cycles.

148
00:16:37,222 --> 00:16:45,990
20 percent of the L 2 accesses also go to memory, at 100 cycles. Point 2 times 100 is 20.

149
00:16:46,440 --> 00:16:51,325
So t average of L 2 is 10 plus 20, which is 30 cycles.

150
00:16:51,775 --> 00:16:59,861
An L 1 miss gets the data from L 2. So t miss of L 1 is t average of L 2: 30 cycles.

151
00:17:00,311 --> 00:17:05,217
Now L 1. Every access pays t hit of L 1: 1 cycle.

152
00:17:05,667 --> 00:17:16,185
10 percent of the accesses also pay t miss of L 1. Point 1 times 30 is 3 cycles: the 30-cycle bar shrinks to a tenth.

153
00:17:16,635 --> 00:17:20,624
So t average is 1 plus 3, which is 4 cycles.

154
00:17:21,074 --> 00:17:30,738
The 3 miss cycles have two parts. Every L 1 miss pays t hit of L 2, 10 cycles. Point 1 times 10 is 1 cycle.

155
00:17:31,188 --> 00:17:40,532
The 2 accesses that go to memory also pay 100 cycles. Point 1 times point 2 times 100 is 2 cycles.

156
00:17:40,982 --> 00:17:47,318
So the miss part is 1 plus 2, which is 3 cycles: the same 3 as in the bar above.

157
00:17:47,768 --> 00:18:02,851
Check with the 100 accesses. 100 times 1, plus 10 times 10, plus 2 times 100, is 400 cycles. 400 divided by 100 is 4 cycles per access.

158
00:18:03,301 --> 00:18:11,109
Assignment 5, question 3. The I cache miss rate is 4 percent, and the D cache miss rate is 6 percent.

159
00:18:11,559 --> 00:18:15,207
Loads and stores are 40 percent of the instructions.

160
00:18:15,657 --> 00:18:18,686
Without memory stalls, the CPI is 2.

161
00:18:19,136 --> 00:18:21,355
Every miss costs 80 cycles.

162
00:18:21,805 --> 00:18:28,205
The question: how much faster is a processor with a perfect cache. Pause the video and try it.

163
00:18:28,655 --> 00:18:35,631
Count the misses per instruction. Every instruction is fetched from the I cache: 1 access per instruction.

164
00:18:36,081 --> 00:18:41,329
4 percent of these accesses miss: point 0 4 misses per instruction.

165
00:18:41,779 --> 00:18:47,240
Only loads and stores use the D cache: point 4 accesses per instruction.

166
00:18:47,690 --> 00:18:55,647
6 percent of these miss. Point 4 times point 0 6 is point 0 2 4 misses per instruction.

167
00:18:56,097 --> 00:19:04,033
Add the misses. Point 0 4 plus point 0 2 4 is point 0 6 4 misses per instruction.

168
00:19:04,483 --> 00:19:12,633
Each miss costs 80 cycles. Point 0 6 4 times 80 is 5.12 stall cycles per instruction.

169
00:19:13,083 --> 00:19:17,819
With a perfect cache, there are no stalls. The CPI is the base, 2.

170
00:19:18,269 --> 00:19:24,178
The real machine adds the I cache stalls. Point 0 4 times 80 is 3.2.

171
00:19:24,628 --> 00:19:31,049
It also adds the D cache stalls. Point 0 2 4 times 80 is 1.92.

172
00:19:31,499 --> 00:19:38,006
The real CPI is 2 plus 3.2 plus 1.92, which is 7.12.

173
00:19:38,456 --> 00:19:47,117
The two stall parts, 3.2 plus 1.92, are 5.12: the same stall cycles as above.

174
00:19:47,567 --> 00:20:07,663
The instruction count and the clock rate are the same. So the time ratio is the CPI ratio. 7.12 divided by 2 is 3.56: the base bar fits 3.56 times into the real bar. The perfect cache is 3.56 times faster.

175
00:20:08,113 --> 00:20:21,340
The solution file writes point 2 4 for the D cache misses. That is a typo for point 0 2 4. Its final numbers, 5.12 and 3.56, are right.

176
00:20:21,790 --> 00:20:38,686
Spring 2020 quiz 2, question 1. For memory instructions, 60 percent hit in L 1, at 5 cycles. 20 percent hit in L 2, at 10 cycles. 20 percent go to local memory, at 300 cycles.

177
00:20:39,136 --> 00:20:48,779
The other instructions: arithmetic and logical instructions are 50 percent, at 4 cycles. Branches are 10 percent, at 2 cycles.

178
00:20:49,229 --> 00:20:59,383
Part A asks for the CPI of the memory instructions. Part B asks for the overall CPI. Pause the video and try parts A and B.

179
00:20:59,833 --> 00:21:07,684
Assume that each latency is the total time of an access served at that level. State the assumption in the exam.

180
00:21:08,134 --> 00:21:17,371
Part A is a weighted average over the levels. The strip shows where the memory instructions are served: 60, 20 and 20 percent.

181
00:21:17,821 --> 00:21:23,325
L 1 serves 60 percent at 5 cycles. Point 6 times 5 is 3.

182
00:21:23,775 --> 00:21:28,959
L 2 serves 20 percent at 10 cycles. Point 2 times 10 is 2.

183
00:21:29,409 --> 00:21:36,129
Local memory serves 20 percent at 300 cycles. Point 2 times 300 is 60.

184
00:21:36,579 --> 00:21:47,353
Add the parts. 3 plus 2 plus 60 is 65. So the CPI of the memory instructions is 65. Memory dominates the bar.

185
00:21:47,803 --> 00:22:01,243
Part B. Arithmetic and logical instructions are 50 percent, and branches are 10 percent. Memory instructions are the rest: 100 minus 50 minus 10 is 40 percent.

186
00:22:01,693 --> 00:22:04,551
Arithmetic: point 5 times 4 is 2.

187
00:22:05,001 --> 00:22:07,945
Branches: point 1 times 2 is point 2.

188
00:22:08,395 --> 00:22:14,155
Memory: point 4 times 65, the CPI from part A, is 26.

189
00:22:14,605 --> 00:22:20,536
The overall CPI is 2 plus point 2 plus 26, which is 28.2.

190
00:22:20,986 --> 00:22:28,175
Part C, 4 marks. An optimization makes arithmetic and logical instructions 2 times faster.

191
00:22:28,625 --> 00:22:36,689
But half of the memory instructions that went to local memory now go to remote memory, at 450 cycles.

192
00:22:37,139 --> 00:22:41,641
The question: does program P benefit. Pause the video and try it.

193
00:22:42,091 --> 00:22:47,787
First, the new memory CPI. L 1 and L 2 do not change: 3 and 2.

194
00:22:48,237 --> 00:22:55,618
Half of the 20 percent local accesses stay local: 10 percent. Point 1 times 300 is 30.

195
00:22:56,068 --> 00:23:02,724
The other 10 percent go to remote memory. Point 1 times 450 is 45.

196
00:23:03,174 --> 00:23:10,854
The memory CPI is 3 plus 2 plus 30 plus 45, which is 80. Before, it was 65.

197
00:23:11,304 --> 00:23:20,243
Now the overall CPI. Arithmetic takes 4 divided by 2, which is 2 cycles. Point 5 times 2 is 1.

198
00:23:20,693 --> 00:23:24,639
Branches do not change. Point 1 times 2 is point 2.

199
00:23:25,089 --> 00:23:28,012
Memory: point 4 times 80 is 32.

200
00:23:28,462 --> 00:23:33,966
The new CPI is 1 plus point 2 plus 32, which is 33.2.

201
00:23:34,416 --> 00:23:38,491
The old CPI, from parts A and B, is 28.2.

202
00:23:38,941 --> 00:23:43,293
The arithmetic part saves 2 minus 1, which is 1 cycle.

203
00:23:43,743 --> 00:23:48,777
But the memory part adds 32 minus 26, which is 6 cycles.

204
00:23:49,227 --> 00:23:58,763
The instruction count and the clock do not change. 33.2 is more than 28.2, so program P does not benefit.

205
00:23:59,213 --> 00:24:06,659
It is slower: the speedup is 28.2 divided by 33.2, which is point 8 5.

206
00:24:07,109 --> 00:24:19,887
The recap. One. Serial access reads one block but is slow. Parallel access is fast but reads all the ways. Way prediction is fast and is about 90 percent accurate.

207
00:24:20,337 --> 00:24:30,577
Two. The 3 C model: compulsory misses are first accesses, capacity misses come after N other blocks, and conflict misses are the rest.

208
00:24:31,027 --> 00:24:46,515
Three. Associativity removes conflict misses. Larger blocks remove compulsory misses but add conflicts. A larger capacity removes capacity misses. Associativity and capacity make t hit longer.

209
00:24:46,965 --> 00:24:51,808
Four. A victim buffer keeps kicked-out blocks and removes conflict misses.

210
00:24:52,258 --> 00:25:03,394
Five. Write-through goes with write-not-allocate. Write-back, with a dirty bit, goes with write-allocate. Write buffers and store buffers hide the write latency.

211
00:25:03,844 --> 00:25:10,116
Six. In a hierarchy, the miss time of a level is the average access time of the next level.

212
00:25:10,566 --> 00:25:23,067
Seven. The CPI is the base CPI plus the misses per instruction times the miss penalty, for each cache. Draw it as a stacked bar. That is the end of the cache videos.
